  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 318 AM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nThe main concern this forecast period continues to be the prolonged \nlate season heat. This heat will continue come courtesy of an upper \nlevel ridge which has been dominating the local weather since this \npast weekend. This upper level ridge axis has slid east of the local \narea and is centered over the Tennessee and Ohio River Valleys this \nmorning. This will keep temperatures similar to yesterday with highs \nin the mid to upper 90s. Moisture, however, will increase slightly \ntoday with dewpoints rising into the mid 60s to low 70s which will \nyield dewpoints in the 100-107 range. Tonight into tomorrow, the \nridge will begin to retrograde back westward.  This will provide \nsubtle height rises which may allow temperatures to rise a degree or \ntwo with dewpoints remaining in the mid 60s to lower 70s which will \nagain yield heat index values in the 100-107 range. Saturday, the \nridge axis will build over the local area driving highs into the mid \n90s to over 100. Slightly lower dewpoints will again keep heat \nindices in the 100-107.\n\nSunday, a couple stronger upper level shortwaves will round the \nridge acting to temporarily flatten it and bring the chance (15-30%) \nfor a few showers and thunderstorms to the northern CWA. This will \nallow temperatures to \"cool\" a few degrees across the northern CWA \nto the lower 90s while over the southern CWA temperatures will still \nachieve the upper 90s to near 100. Monday and Tuesday the upper \nlevel ridge reasserts itself over the area prolonging the heat with \nhighs remaining in the mid to upper 90s. Model differences exist \nbeyond Tuesday that lends uncertainty to the potential for \nprecipitation in the Tuesday night through Wednesday timeframe. The \nGFS through this period keeps the upper level ridge entrenched over \nthe area as a upper level trough moves through the Canadian Plains \nkeeping precipitation north of the area. The EC is stronger and \nfurther south with the upper level trough across the northern tier \nstates flattening the upper ridge over the area and bringing he \nchance for precipitation. \n\nIn regards to the heat advisory that is currently in effect through \nSaturday evening, it may need to be extended into early next week. \nAnd, due to the duration of the heat being greater than for days it \nmay need to be converted into a extreme heat warning with future \nforecast updates.",
